<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="size-full wp-image-1128220" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083915/19.jpg.jpeg" alt width="1500" height="1003" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083915/19.jpg.jpeg 1500w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083915/19.jpg-360x241.jpeg 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083915/19.jpg-1024x685.jpeg 1024w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083915/19.jpg-240x160.jpeg 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083915/19.jpg-768x514.jpeg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1500px) 100vw, 1500px"></p>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Lloyd_Wright">Frank Lloyd Wright</a> was often strapped for cash. In that respect, he had something in common with Vincent van Gogh, who passed most of his much shorter life in more dire circumstances. Unlike Wright, who enjoyed decades of fame at the pinnacle of the American architectural profession, van Gogh remained practically unknown until after his death. But if they’ve encountered one another in the afterlife, they’ve certainly discussed one subject above all others: their shared appreciation for Japanese woodblock prints. Here on Open Culture, we’ve previously featured <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2019/01/download-vincent-van-goghs-collection-of-japanese-prints.html">the collection of 500 such works of art that van Gogh left behind</a>. At the end of Wright’s life in 1959, he owned about 6,000, many of which his estate had to sell to pay both Japanese art dealers and American tax collectors.</p>
<p>We could say that Wright died as he lived, advanced age having not evidently diminished either his financial recklessness or his obsession with <em>ukiyo‑e</em>. The masters of that classical “art of the floating world” like <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2019/01/download-vincent-van-goghs-collection-of-japanese-prints.html">Hokusai</a> and <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2021/06/download-1000-beautiful-woodblock-prints-by-hiroshige.html">Hiroshige</a> were among his favorite artists of any kind. When still a teenager, Wright went to work for the architect <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Lyman_Silsbee">Joseph Silsbee,</a> who’d built a chapel for his uncle.</p>
