<p>“Silsbee’s cousin, Ernest Fenollosa, happened to be the world’s leading Western expert on Japanese art at the time,” <a href="https://theconversation.com/frank-lloyd-wrights-japanese-education-78380?xid=PS_smithsonian">writes Kevin Nute at The Conversation</a>. It isn’t certain how well the young Wright knew Fenollosa — or indeed when he began collecting <em>ukiyo‑e</em>, writes <em>Frank Lloyd Wright and the Art of Japan</em> author Julia Meech-Pekarik, though he may have seen both Hokusai and Hiroshige’s works at the World’s Columbian Exposition in Chicago in 1893, “about the time he opened his practice in Oak Park.”</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128222" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083956/default-5.jpg" alt width="843" height="1241" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083956/default-5.jpg 843w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083956/default-5-245x360.jpg 245w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083956/default-5-696x1024.jpg 696w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083956/default-5-163x240.jpg 163w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08083956/default-5-768x1131.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 843px) 100vw, 843px"></p>
<p>Eight years later, “Wright made his first voyage to Japan in pursuit of prints, and he did indeed return with enough expertise to stage an exhibition of his own Hiroshige collection at the Art Institute of Chicago in March of 1906.” He returned for two months in 1913, making it clear that he was only interested in using that time to acquire more prints. Later, while working on the <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2022/07/a-virtual-tour-of-frank-lloyd-wrights-lost-japanese-masterpiece-the-imperial-hotel-in-tokyo.html">Imperial Hotel</a> between 1917 and 1922, he spent a total of more than three years there, later writing that “the pursuit of the Japanese print became my constant recreation while in Tokio,” a quest whose “adventures and excursions would take place at night or sometimes call for a journey by day to distant places.” This positioned him well to take on a sideline as a dealer (or as he liked to call himself, a merchant) of Japanese art in a United States only just becoming aware of its beauty — an especially advantageous condition in architecturally lean years.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128223" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08084021/3014.008.webp" alt width="1440" height="981" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08084021/3014.008.webp 1440w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08084021/3014.008-360x245.webp 360w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08084021/3014.008-1024x698.webp 1024w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08084021/3014.008-240x164.webp 240w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/09/08084021/3014.008-768x523.webp 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px"></p>
<p>Wright valued&nbsp;<em>ukiyo‑e</em>, about which he published a book in 1912, for its sense of order, structure, nature, and simplicity. “Five lines where three are enough is stupidity,” he wrote. “Nine pounds where three are sufficient is stupidity. But to eliminate expressive words that intensify or vivify meaning in speaking or writing is not simplicity.” In his own form, he understood full well that “expressive changes of surface, emphasis of line, and especially textures of material, may go to make facts eloquent — forms more significant.” But it was Hokusai, Hiroshige, and countless other commercial artists of that earlier time and distant land that changed his approach to building in the first place. “When I saw the print and I saw the elimination of the insignificant and simplicity of vision, together with the sense of rhythm and the importance of design, I began to see nature in a totally different way.” And thus, thanks to Japanese art, did that most American of all architects begin taking shape.</p>
